    here; I'll put a one-line comment in the HTML saying so, since that's the one non-obvious thing a future reader can
    break silently.

  No changes to [38;5;153msrc/[39m. Nothing in the Node half reads the API endpoint, and wiring it in would be speculative.

  [1m3. Error handling[0m

  [38;5;153mresolveEnvironment[39m throws on an unmapped hostname with a message that names the offending hostname and lists the known
  ones — an unlisted host should be diagnosable from the console in one read, not require opening the source.

  The wrinkle worth calling out: classic [38;5;153m<script>[39m tags are independent, so a throw in [38;5;153msettings.js[39m does [1mnot[0m stop [38;5;153mapp.js[39m
  from running. Left alone, you'd get the real error at load and then a confusing [38;5;153mCannot read properties of undefined[39m on
  submit. So the submit handler gets one guard — if [38;5;153mwindow.AppSettings[39m is missing, log "settings failed to load, see
  the earlier error" and return without submitting. Two errors, both pointing at the real cause, and the form refuses to
  submit to an unknown endpoint. That's the "fail loudly" decision actually holding at the point where it matters.

  [1m4. Testing — and a question[0m

  This repo has [1mno test infrastructure at all[0m: no test script, no dependencies, no runner. That's why I made
  [38;5;153mresolveEnvironment[39m pure — it's the piece worth testing (known host maps correctly, unknown host throws), and it's
  testable with zero DOM.

  Setting up tooling is cheapest before code exists, so:
